## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit, with `ThreadAbortException`/`ResetAbort` as control flow. All three APIs throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+.

### Why It Breaks

| Removed API | .NET 8 Behavior |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `ThreadAbortException` catch | Never triggered (abort can't happen) |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the
            // token fires, even if the delegate itself doesn't check it.
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Changes

1. **`CancellationTokenSource(timeout)`** — auto-cancels after the deadline, replacing the manual `Join` + `Abort` pattern.
2. **`Task.Run`** — replaces raw `Thread` creation. Runs on the thread pool.
3. **`Task.WaitAsync(token)`** (.NET 6+) — returns immediately with `OperationCanceledException` when the token fires, even if the work is still running.
4. **`OperationCanceledException`** — replaces the `ThreadAbortException` catch.

### ⚠️ Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Details |
|---|---|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | `Task.WaitAsync` abandons the *wait*, not the *work*. The delegate keeps running on the thread pool. If `work` is CPU-bound and never checks a token, it runs to completion in the background. |
| **To truly stop work, pass the token** | Overload to `Func<CancellationToken, T>` and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside loops or between expensive steps. |
| **Blocking I/O won't respond to cancellation** | Sync calls like `Thread.Sleep` or `socket.Receive` ignore the token. Use `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne()` or async I/O instead. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures only timeout-caused cancellations become `TimeoutException`. Other cancellations propagate. |
| **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` owns a timer — always wrap in `using` to avoid leaks. |

### Fully Cooperative Version (Recommended)

If callers can pass cancellation-aware work:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    // Preferred: work cooperates with cancellation
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            return Task.Run(() => work(token), token)
                       .WaitAsync(token)
                       .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Backward-compatible overload (work runs unaware of cancellation)
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        return ExecuteWithTimeout(_ => work(), timeout);
    }
}
```

Callers then write:

```csharp
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return ComputeResult();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

This ensures the work **actually stops** at the next checkpoint when the timeout fires.
